`mcp-live-toggles`  Make an MCP switch take effect without restarting Strata. [py] [ui] DONE 2026-11-19
    ASKED FOR by the human after the chips shipped ("is there any way we can make it take effect without
    restarting?").  The obstacle is not the process, it is the prompt: the tools block is the FIRST system turn
    (serve/chat_template.jinja, the `if tools` branch), and reuse is exact token-prefix matching, so changing the
    set changes the start of every conversation's prompt and costs it a full re-read - measured here at
    328,811 prompt tokens for the live chat, roughly 3 minutes at this box's read rate.  Nothing about the engine
    needs restarting: the list is fetched per request (server.py, `extra = svc.mcp.template_tools(...)`).
    DONE, the "frozen per chat" shape, chosen with the human:
      - POST /mcp now also updates the running hub (McpHub.set_tool_disabled / set_server_disabled): a tool
        toggle re-routes at once; a server going off is closed and dropped, one coming back is constructed and
        started on its own thread.  New conversations see the change with no restart.
      - A conversation that already has the old list in its prompt sends it: the request key `strata_mcp_tools`
        (server.py, mcp_only) filters what template_tools renders, and `include_disabled` lets that same request
        still CALL a tool the config has since turned off - a tool the model can see but cannot run is a broken
        turn.  run_with_mcp carries `frozen` for that.
      - The page keeps the list per chat in memory only (app.js, mcpFrozen / mcpChatTools).  The human asked for
        the reload to reset it: after a reload the chat uses the current list and pays the one re-read.  A
        "Use the current tools in this chat" button does the same without a reload.  Nothing is written into the
        chat record, so a chat carried across a reload is not pinned to an old tool set.
      - The one change an open chat cannot be shielded from: a whole server going off, whose tools can no longer
        be rendered at all.  POST /mcp answers "reloads": [names] and the toast says those chats will read their
        history once more, instead of letting the next message discover it.
    Tests: serve/test_mcp.py ToolLoop +4 (the request's list is what reaches the prompt; a bad list is a 400
    before anything runs; the chip is live and a frozen chat still RUNS its tool - asserted on ok and the tool's
    text, not just on the events, because an error result looks the same; a server off/on live).  Harness case 91
    drives send(): an open chat keeps its list, a new chat gets the current one, a reload switches it over, and
    the button does the same.  serve/test_mcp_round_history.py's FakeHub.call gained the new kwarg - a double that
    drifts from the real signature hides exactly this.
    Controls: template_tools ignoring `only` fails the prompt check; hub.call ignoring include_disabled fails the
    frozen-call check (ok: False); dropping body.strata_mcp_tools fails case 91; set_tool_disabled returning
    False fails the live check.  One control had to be strengthened first: asserting only the call/result events
    passed even when the tool errored.
    NOT verified in a browser: the re-read arithmetic (it is the engine's, and the numbers are the measured ones
    above), and what a chat does when a server it used is turned off mid-conversation.
    SUPERSEDED in part by item mcp-chat-tools (same day): the page-session shield (mcpFrozen), the load-time
    baseline (mcpAtLoad) and the status prose are deleted, and the list is on the chat record instead.  The server
    half - strata_mcp_tools, include_disabled, the live hub switches - is what that item builds on.
    POLISH from the human's read of the card (2026-11-19): the chip now shows only the tool's own name - the
    server's name is already the card it sits under, and `easyCMP__` on every chip was noise - with the full
    model-facing name in the tooltip beside the description.  The "use the current tools" button names the chat
    it applies to ("Use the current tools in \"Refactor the parser\"") and only EXISTS when that chat is holding
    a list different from what the server offers now (mcpSwitchState): a chat that has not sent in this page load
    is not shielded, and a chat whose list already matches has nothing to switch to, so in both cases there is no
    button at all and a status line says which state it is in ("will use the current tools (2) on its next
    message" / "already uses the current tools (2)" / "is holding the 2 tools it started with; the server now
    offers 1").  Greyed-out was tried first and failed in the browser: the human reloads the page for every
    web-app change, so they always landed in the not-shielded state and the button looked permanently broken.
    The state is re-evaluated whenever a different chat comes on screen (showChat calls renderMcp) and whenever a
    chat sends (send calls renderMcp), which is what makes it right for several chats rather than one.  The long
    explanation moved into the tooltip; the visible line is one sentence.
    Tests: harness case 92 (the chip label, the tooltip, no button before a send, the button appearing for the
    shielded chat, the status line naming whichever chat is on screen).  Controls: rendering the button always
    fails the "no button at all" check; dropping renderMcp() from send() leaves the status line stale and fails;
    dropping it from showChat fails the per-chat check.
    FIXED 2026-11-20 (the human, once the Settings tab shipped): the cost note went stale.  "This re-reads
    328,811 tokens in this conversation" stayed on screen after the conversation had sent a message and paid it.
    The note is a promise about the NEXT message, so the page now remembers, per chat and in memory only, the
    tool-list signature the last turn used (mcpPaid, mcpSignature - captured when the turn starts, recorded when
    it ends), and mcpCostText() stays empty while the open chat's paid signature equals the current list.  Not
    one-way: a new change makes a new promise and the number comes back.  Harness case 96 drives it - switch, the
    number; send, silence; switch again, the number back - and four controls fail it (not recording the signature,
