`autoscroll-cooldown`  Auto-scroll should back off when the reader fights it. [ui] DONE 2026-11-19
    DONE: the follow rule (nearBottom(), 120 px of slack) is now gated on a hold.  A scroll listener on #chat-scroll
    compares the chat's position against autoTop - where stickBottom LEFT it, read back after the assignment,
    because a real browser clamps scrollTop to the bottom - and a position more than 24 px above that is the
    reader fighting, so follow is held off for 2500 ms.  Coming back to the bottom clears it immediately; a forced
    scroll (renderChat, a chat switch) clears it too.  paint() and the final paint both use following().
    A comparison against intent, not a consumed flag: the page's own scroll fires the same event (LESSONS.md).
    Tests: serve/test_chat_sidebar.js case 89 drives send() with a growing answer - stick, stick, reader up 100 px
    (inside the 120 px slack), frame does not drag, reader back down, follow resumes.  Control: following() without
    the hold fails the "does not drag them back" assertion.
    NOT measured in a browser: the stub never clamps scrollTop, so the read-back (autoTop = s.scrollTop, not
    scrollHeight) is reasoned from how a real browser behaves - if the follow ever switches itself off for good,
    that line is the first suspect.

`test-suite-speed`  The suites spend almost all their time asleep, not working. [py] [ui] DONE 2026-11-19
    Measured 2026-11-19 on this box: the fast suite was 194 tests in 43.6 s, but the node harness was 7.97 s wall
    against 0.30 s CPU (3%), and every test in serve/test_static_cache cost a uniform ~520 ms.  Two causes:
      - serve/test_chat_sidebar.js awaited setTimeout(r, 30) 228 times = 6.8 s of sleep.  It now yields with
        setImmediate, which still lands after the microtasks the stub IndexedDB and stub fetch answer on: the
        harness is 7.97 s -> 0.19 s.
      - serve() ran httpd.serve_forever() with its default poll_interval of 0.5 s, so every shutdown() in every
        test waited out a poll.  serve() now passes poll_interval 0.02 (serve/server.py, serve()).  Fast suite
        36.8 s -> 9.4 s; serve.test_server 94.6 s -> 54.7 s (317 tests, both OK).  For the running server the
        same line means a restart notices in <=20 ms instead of <=500 ms, and 50 idle wakeups a second instead
        of 2 - negligible against one request, but it is a production default, so it needs the human's restart.
    Still open: the remaining time is real work (scripted engine delays, watchdog and lifecycle tests), and
    67 serve(port=0) call sites across 27 test files mean the modules could run in parallel - that is item
    test-command's shape to decide (sh vs py vs bat), not a speed fix.

`drop-single-chat`  Kill the "Chat history" switch: the list is always the page. [ui] DONE 2026-11-19
    DONE (the human's call: "no one should ever use it"): the About switch, the `historyOn` flag and every
    off-branch are gone - the one-chat-in-localStorage mode, the New-chat-clears-it-with-an-Undo path, and the
    `keepBytes=false` argument that only that path used (a switch nothing can set is a switch that gets misread
    later).  What SURVIVES is the migration: openHistory still reads an old "strata.chat" key and turns it into
    the first record, so a browser that only ever had the single chat does not open on an empty list.
    A browser that refuses IndexedDB (private mode) keeps working with the list in memory and still says so
    (openHistory's warnStorage).
    Tests: serve/test_chat_sidebar.py rewrote six checks that pinned the off mode (the page markup, "opt-in and
    off is the last release" -> "the mode is gone and its key is migrated", "the store is only used when the
    switch is on" -> "opened at start, memory-only still works", "turning the switch off keeps the chat on screen"
    -> "New chat never clears the chat you are in", the attachment check, the draft check).  Harness case 1
    rewritten the same way, case 11 (flip the switch off) deleted; 85 -> 84 ok lines.
    Controls: putting `hidden` back on #sidebar fails the page check; writing to "strata.chat" again fails the
    migration check; and the removal caught one real bug on its own - start-up called openHistory(false) without
    the .then(renderChat) that setHistory used to do, so the list was read and never drawn (harness: "the list
    shows them - 0 !== 4").

`unread-call-instrument`  Say out loud when a tool call the model wrote never became a call. [py] SMALL DONE 2026-11-19
    DONE: OutputParser counts it (`unread`, `unread_names`) and serve/server.py prints one line and adds
    `totals.unread_tool_calls`, so /metrics sees it too.  Nothing about what the parser DELIVERS changed - the
    markup still comes back as text - and every existing test still passed unchanged (fast suite 201 -> 210,
    serve.test_server 317 OK, the page harnesses 85 ok lines and 37 tests).
    Four shapes counted, each tested with a control in serve/test_reasoning_rescue.py class UnreadCall (9 cases):
    a call inside a code fence that never closes (the one that killed several turns of one agent chat: the answer
    ends with the call as prose, and NO counter fired - not abandoned, the envelope closed; not refused, the name
    was never reached; not an empty turn, text was delivered); the same inside the thinking; an undeclared name in
    the thinking; and the JSON form naming an undeclared tool.  A fence that closes, inline code, and prose that
    names the format are quotes and stay uncounted - that is its own test, so the number is not noise.
    The count and the name do not depend on how the text arrives (whole, or one character per delta).
    Not in the Monitor's per-request record, on purpose: history.append in Service.run runs before
    parser.finish(), and the fence case is only decided there - a field would read absent for the very shape it
    flags (LESSONS.md).  The printed line and totals.unread_tool_calls are what carry it.
    Needs a restart to take effect (serve/server.py).  The next time a turn dies this way the log names the tool
    and says which of the two causes it was: a name the request did not declare, or a form the template does not
    use.  What to do about it is item unread-call-recovery.

`markdown-cheatsheet-gaps`  What the page's markdown does not render, measured against a cheatsheet. [ui] SMALL
    Reviewed 2026-11-20 against https://github.com/im-luka/markdown-cheatsheet by running every construct in it
    through the page's own markdown() / inline() under the node harness stub - not by reading the renderer.
    Works: paragraphs, headings, **bold**, *italic*, _italic_, ***both***, blockquotes, inline code, fenced code
    with a language and a copy button, inline links, ordered and unordered lists (- and *), tables with alignment,
    *** rules, and escaping (5 < 6 & 7 > 2 stays safe).  Raw HTML is NOT passed through - the page escapes first,
    and that is the security posture, not a gap.
    FIXED 2026-11-20 (asked: "words like __still__ are still wrong"):
      - __bold__ - the pair of the _emphasis_ rule, same guards, so strata__mcp__tools stays literal and a code
        span stays as typed
      - ~~strikethrough~~ -> del
      - the rule regex: it was ^\s*([-*_])\s*\1\s*\1[\s\1]*$ and [\s\1] inside a character class is
        whitespace-or-the-control-character \x01, NOT a backreference, so only a run of exactly three matched.
        Now ^\s{0,3}([-*_])(?:[ \t]*\1){2,}[ \t]*$ : ---, -----, _____, ***, ****, "- - -" and "_ _ _" are rules,
        "--" and "-" are not.
    Pinned in serve/test_chat_sidebar.js case 92; four controls fail it (drop either new rule, put the old regex
    back, loosen the identifier guard).  Page-only: a reload, no restart.
    Still does not:
      - images: ![a](url) renders as a LINK with a literal ! in front of it - there is no img path at all
      - link and image titles: [t](url "T") renders the whole thing as literal text
      - reference links and images: [t][r] with the [r]: url definition
      - in-page anchors: [go](#some-id) renders as literal text - the link rule wants a scheme
      - task lists: - [x] done shows the brackets
      - nested lists: an indented item under a numbered one flattens to a sibling
      - an ordered list written 1. 2. 7. loses the 7
      - double-backtick inline code (two backticks around `x`) mangles
    Footnotes ([^1]) are GitHub-specific and probably out of scope for a chat.  Bare URLs are already item
    auto-link-urls.  The decision left to the owner: an img path means the page fetches whatever URL the model
    writes - worth a rule (attachments already render pictures the user put there); titles, reference links and
    footnotes look like docs features a chat does not owe.

`crash-recovers-last-turn`  A browser crash mid-turn loses the whole last exchange. [ui] SMALL
    The chat record is written once, at the end of send() (saveChat after the stream closes). If the tab dies -
    crash, OOM, the machine sleeping - the user's own message, the thinking, and every tool call that already
    ran are gone. Write the record as each part finishes: when the message is sent, when the first token
    arrives, when thinking ends, after each tool result, and at the end. The conversations are one IndexedDB
    record per chat and can be megabytes, so this needs a cheap path: write on those boundaries only, never
    per token (the composer draft already stays out of the record for that reason - store/localStorage).
    Decide what a reloaded half-turn looks like: the answer so far with a note that the page was closed, and
    whether the tool calls that ran are replayed or shown as spent.

`sidebar-spinner-states`  The chat-list spinner: prefill blue, generating green, queued amber, waiting grey -
    and an option to show it on the open chat too. [ui] SMALL
    Today the dot is hidden for the chat you are looking at, and one colour covers the whole turn. The states
    are already known to the page (live.state: reading / generating / running-tool / queued, plus this tab's
    own busy), and the palette already encodes them: --st-accent green = the engine is producing tokens,
    --st-info blue = it is not (a prompt read, an MCP call), --st-warn amber = queued (components.css, and
    LESSONS.md "one colour per state" - a pill that disagreed with the Monitor's badge is what that entry is
    about). So: blue while reading the prompt, green once the first token lands, amber when the request is
    waiting behind another, grey when it is waiting on something outside the engine (a tool call, an MCP
    server). Add a Settings switch to always show it, off by default - it is hidden now because the open chat
    already shows the pill.

`unpark-chat`  Un-park a parked conversation by hand. [py] SMALL
    Ask for by the owner 2026-11-21 as a future item, independent of pin-conversation. Parking a conversation
    (the live conversation cache) is automatic today; there is no way to say "drop this one, I do not want its
    slot" - which matters because the engine keeps only 4 conversation-cache slots (measured on this box:
    conversation_cache_slots 4, conversation_cache_mib 16384), so a chat you no longer care about is holding
    room another one needs. Needs an identity for the conversation first - reuse is exact token-prefix
    matching with no session id (see edit-last-message-reread and pin-conversation).

`edit-last-message-reread`  Why does editing the LAST message re-read the whole chat? Measure it. [py] SMALL
    It should not: the edit truncates the history to that turn and re-sends (serve/web/app.js, send(editOf)),
    so everything before it is byte-identical and the prompt should reuse up to the edited turn. Two
    candidates, and they need different fixes: (1) the engine keeps only 4 conversation-cache slots
    (conversation_cache_slots: 4 on this box), so this chat's prefix may simply have been evicted - in which
    case the re-read has nothing to do with editing and every chat beyond four pays it; (2) the replay differs
    somewhere before the edited turn, which is the class of bug that produced the 36,281-token re-read fixed
    on 2026-11-21 (item unread-call-recovery) - the tell is the reused/prompt_read pair in
    /metrics?requests=all: a break at the tools block re-reads everything, a break at the tail re-reads a few
    hundred tokens. Measure before touching anything.

## 3. Medium - two or three layers, or a decision to make first (days)

`trim-tool-results`  Trim long tool calls before they reach the DOM. [py] [ui] - decide the policy first
    The question "do we trim?" has a measured answer for this box: `max_result_chars` is
    **500,000** in strata-iq3_s.json (the code default is 20,000), and `max_rounds` is 900.
    So one call can put 500 KB of text into the page, and a long agent turn can do that many
    times. The DOM cap is only visual: `.tool-call__pre` has `max-height: 320px; overflow:
    auto` (app.css:113), which bounds the *box*, not the text node inside it.
    So yes, this can overwhelm the page, and it is partly mitigated already: an open block's
    body is built lazily and only while open (syncToolBody), so a closed call costs nothing.
    The exposure is (a) a call the reader opens, and (b) every result kept in the message
    object and written to IndexedDB.
    Options, in order of preference:
      - cap what goes into the DOM per open block (say 100 KB) with an explicit
        "showing the first 100 KB of 500 KB - Download / Copy full" affordance. Keeps the
        full text for copy and export, bounds the render.
      - keep only a preview in the message object and store the full result once, keyed by
        call id, fetched on demand. Bigger change, but it is the one that fixes IndexedDB
        growth too.
      - lower `max_result_chars` in the config. Cheapest, but it changes what the MODEL
        sees, which is a different decision from what the browser renders - do not conflate
        them.
    Measure before building: how big is a real chat record here, and how many open blocks
    does a 900-round turn actually produce?

`import-export`  Import and export conversations. [ui] [py] DONE 2026-10-24
    DONE: a Settings gear in the sidebar opens a modal with Export / Import / Delete conversations.  All three use
    one chat-picker: a checkbox per chat (default on), a name filter, a date range (from-only = everything on/after;
    to-only = everything on/before; both = the range), and All/None that act on the VISIBLE rows - but the action
    applies to every CHECKED chat even if the current filter hides it.  Format is llama.cpp-compatible JSONL (one
    chat per line, OpenAI-shaped `messages`) PLUS a `strata` block carrying ids, time, reasoning, tool rounds with
    at/rat, files and usage, so thinking + tool rounds round-trip (a pure llama.cpp file would lose them and break
    the replay - the note below).  Import parses both (with or without the extras).  The sidebar now shows each
    chat's size and attachment count.
    DONE (also): a global "Enable attachments" toggle (default on) that gates the composer's attach button and
    addFiles - surfaced inside the Export and Import pickers.  The Delete picker has a "Delete only attachments
    (keep the chats)" toggle at the bottom that routes the action to strip attachments vs delete the chats.  The
    picker's bottom button is "Back" (returns to the Chat settings menu); Escape and backdrop-click still close.
    Size/attachment count shows in the picker rows (not the sidebar list).
    Note: tool rounds carry `at` and `rat` offsets that the replay depends on (assistantMessages, and
    serve/server.py on why they must match). An import that loses them re-creates the re-read bug - hence the
    `strata` block round-trips them; a strictly-llama.cpp import can't preserve them.
    FIXED (import of real llama.cpp exports): the importer now handles all three shapes - the NEW export (a .zip of
    per-conversation .jsonl: a session header + a message TREE with parent/children + currNode, toolCalls and
    toolCallId, reasoningContent), the OLD export (a .json array of {conv, messages}), and our own .jsonl.  It walks
    the active branch (currNode -> parents, reversed), drops system/root messages, groups assistant+tool runs into
    Strata turns with at/rat, and restores thinking from reasoningContent.  Zip is read with the browser's
    DecompressionStream (deflate-raw).  Verified against the real 2026-10-26 export (a 282-message tool-heavy
    branch groups with every tool result matched).
    FIXED (attachment chats not listed): the picker called chatSize -> JSON.stringify on every chat, and 800+ huge
    chats froze the list.  chatSize is now a cheap sum of string lengths.  Also mapped llama.cpp's `extra`
    attachments (TEXT -> file, IMAGE -> image with base64Url) so imported chats keep their attachments.  Added a
    "Zip (one .jsonl per chat)" toggle to Export (writes a STORE-method zip via crc32, round-trips through the
    importer).  Raised the toast layer above the modals so import warnings are readable.

`edit-posts`  Edit and delete posts in a conversation, mine and the model's. [ui] [py] PARTLY DONE 2026-10-24
    PROGRESS (built in increments, decided with the human 2026-10-24):
      - DONE (increment 1): message ids (mid(), backfilled on load, identity only - never sent, so the replay is
        untouched); an Edit button on each post (openEditBox, an inline textarea); Save edits the text in place and
        keeps every message below it (the "edit only, do not resubmit" path); Cancel/Escape confirm only when the
        text changed; a turn that made tool calls is LOCKED (canEditMsg) because its rounds are offsets into its own
        text (at/rat) and editing that breaks the replay.
      - DONE (increment 2): Submit on a user post - send(editOf) saves the edit, drops every message after it, and
        resends a fresh turn; the box shows Save / Submit / Cancel for user posts and Save / Cancel for assistant
        posts.  (The inline re-read warning is not shown yet.)
      - DONE (increment 3): the tool-call edit modal.  Editing a turn that made calls opens a mode menu (a fixed
        popover, like the row menu) with "Edit final output only" (keeps the tool rounds and their at/rat offsets,
        edits only the text after the last call), "Edit entire (drops its tool calls)" (replaces the text, clears
        m.tools), or Cancel.  The Edit button is now offered on every post.
      - TODO: the inline re-read warning on Submit; keyboard consolidation (see configurable-send-keys).
      - DONE (delete-a-post): a Delete button on every post opens a modal (not a bare confirm) with a "delete all
        messages below" checkbox - default ON (truncate at that message, the safe case), or OFF to remove just that
        one (leaves the turns after it).  It warns the action is undoable only for the session and will re-read the
        conversation from there (~N tokens).  Blocked while a turn runs, and it discards an open edit first.
      - FIXED (streaming + submit polish): tool calls count in the live meta as soon as they start (not only after
        they finish); the meta row stays visible during a turn so stats show while thinking, not only once output
        arrives; the new turn's row is marked .streaming immediately so the Edit button never flickers; and Submit
        now opens a resend-confirm modal (confirmResend) like delete - it drops everything after the edited post
        and re-reads, so it should not fire on a stray click.
      - FIXED (context accuracy): the poll's live prompt/generated figures are `null` between MCP rounds (shown=
        false when idle), so the message's context collapsed to ~0 in those gaps.  poll() now HOLDS the last
        non-null figures across the gaps (lastLive merge), send() resets them per turn, and liveMeta uses the
        exact `generated` (not a character estimate) so the count no longer jumps at the end.  A stopped turn now
        keeps its token count (from the held live figures) instead of just "Stopped".  The final context is exact
        (from the usage chunk) so it drops the "~"; only the live estimate keeps the "~".
      - FIXED (the "back to 0 tokens" bug): the poll's `generated` counts the CURRENT round only and resets to 0
        when a new round starts after a tool call, so the live count dropped each round.  noteLive() now
        accumulates it across rounds (m._gen, resetting the round baseline when `generated` falls), so the token
        count climbs monotonically for the whole turn.
      - DONE (attachments in the edit box): editing a user post lists its images/files as removable chips plus an
        Attach button (a paperclip, like the composer's; reuses addFiles/renderAttachments with a per-box list).
        Removing and saving updates the post.  Caveat: a reloaded post's attachments are stored name-only, so they
        show as removable chips but the original bytes cannot be re-attached - item store-full-attachments.  Adding
        via the picker is browser-only (FileReader), not unit-tested.
      - FIXED (attachment bugs from the human): saving an edit rebuilds the whole ROW (not just the bubble), so
        thumbnails/chips update without a reload; the Attach button is the paperclip icon; image chips carry a
        thumbnail; the message's own read-only attachment display is hidden while editing (attachments show once,
        as the editable pills with trash icons); clickable chips get a hand cursor; message thumbnails keep their
        aspect ratio and are large.  Clicking a message IMAGE opens it in a new tab via a blob: URL (data: URLs are
        blocked for top-level navigation - the "Not allowed to navigate top frame to data URL" error); clicking a
        text-file chip opens a viewer with its contents; Escape/backdrop close the viewer.  Attachment bytes are now
        kept in the IndexedDB record, so reload no longer loses them (see store-full-attachments).
      - DONE (live meta + guards): a streaming message's meta updates about once a second with an ESTIMATE of
        tokens/rate/tool-calls/context (liveMeta in serve/web/app.js - token count from characters, ~4/word),
        replaced by the exact usage figures when the turn ends; the Edit button is hidden while streaming (the row
        gets a .streaming class, matching the copy button which was already hidden); and deleting a chat now asks
        to confirm first (Undo is still offered for the session).
      - FIXED (bug reports from the human): "Edit entire" left the tool blocks on screen until a reload - the
        redraw now rebuilds the whole bubble, not incrementally; only one edit box at a time (clicking Edit on
        another post asks to discard, a second click on the same post is a no-op, and the editing row hides its
        own Edit button); clicking Edit during a live turn now says "Stop the answer first" instead of doing
        nothing; the edit box scrolls its buttons into view.
      - DONE (option B): editing a turn that has thinking shows a "Delete the model's thinking" checkbox - kept by
        default, checked by default for "Edit entire" (which already drops the tool calls); a user post has none.
        Editing the thinking text itself (option C) stays out: the per-round rat offsets are replay-critical.
      - Assistant posts: Save only (no Submit, no attach).  User posts: Save + Submit + attach.
    Chat-level rename and delete exist (renameChat, deleteChat); message-level delete does not yet.
    Straightforward to build. See item edit-no-reread for the cost.
    The delete wants a modal, not a confirm(): "delete this message", a warning that it
    cannot be undone and that the conversation must be re-read (until item edit-no-reread lands), and a
    checkbox, **checked by default**, "delete all messages below". Unchecked means delete
    only that one and leave the rest - which is the case that changes the prompt mid-stream
    and therefore the one that re-reads everything after it. The modal should say which of
    the two it is about to do, in token terms if the numbers are available (item context-percent already
    computes them).
    Message-level identity is a prerequisite for any of this being safe: messages have no ids
    today, only array position and `time`. See item nvme-park.

`edit-persist-across-chats`  Let an open edit survive switching chats (and a reload). [ui]
    Today switching chats while editing prompts to discard (confirmDiscardEdit in serve/web/app.js) so the edit is
    not silently lost.  The nicer behaviour: keep the edit box open per chat, so you can switch away and back and
    the in-progress edit is still there.  That means storing the draft edit (text + attachments + thinking choice)
    keyed by chat id - in memory for switching, and in the browser (localStorage/IndexedDB) so a reload restores
    it, like the composer draft already does per chat.  Decide whether one edit per chat is enough or several.


`queue-another-chat`  Queue another conversation while one is going (llama.cpp style). [ui] MEDIUM, not engine work
    The SERVER ALREADY DOES THIS: docs/BATCHING.md - "By default Strata serves one request
    at a time: the others wait in the server's queue." Nothing engine-side is missing, and
    --batch N would run them at once instead of queued.
    What is missing is entirely in the page: one busy flag, one send button disabled
    (app.js:1015), one stream reader. Needs per-request state (controller, target message,
    chat id) so two streams can be tracked and painted into the right chat.

`offline-outbox`  Queue a request when the server is down and send it when it is back. [ui] MEDIUM
    Today the page just errors. The SERVER side is already right: requests queue behind the
    FIFO (docs/BATCHING.md - "By default Strata serves one request at a time: the others
    wait in the server's queue"), and a restart never hangs a waiter - it ends with a clean
    503 (serve/test_restart_waiters.py, #1012). So this is entirely client-side.
    Needs: an outbox in IndexedDB (the store already exists), a health poll, ordering rules,
    and a decision about a second message arriving while one is queued. It must survive a
    reload, which is what makes it an IndexedDB problem rather than an array.
    DECIDED 2026-11-21 by the owner: not now.  "I don't think we have a proper queueing system yet. It should
    just be treated exactly the same way it would work if the server was up" - so the page holds a message and
    sends it once the server answers, and the real queue (several waiting, their order, one per chat) stays
    this item.  It cannot be built before item offline-web-assets: with nothing listening on the port the page
    itself does not load, so there is nothing to type into.

`offline-web-assets`  The page opens when nothing is listening on the port: keep the web app in the browser. [ui] [py] DONE 2026-11-21 (needs a restart; never seen in a real browser)
    Asked for 2026-11-21: "the web assets should be totally cached and able to load without the server running".
    Today that is impossible by design, and it is the design we chose: _send_static answers every asset with
    Cache-Control: no-cache plus an ETag (item asset-validator) so an edit to app.js shows on the next reload
    with no restart - which means every load asks the server, and with the server down the navigation itself
    fails.  The conversations survive (IndexedDB); the page does not.  Only a service worker can answer a
    navigation for an origin that is not listening, and none is registered: grep serviceWorker over serve/ finds
    nothing.
    The shape that keeps both promises is network-first while online (so the no-cache dev loop and LESSONS.md's
    "a web-app change needs no restart" stay true) and cache-only when the fetch fails.  What it would hold,
    measured on this box 2026-11-21: index.html 20 KB, web/app.js 208 KB, app.css 36 KB, components.css 12 KB,
    tokens.css 4 KB, favicon.svg 4 KB, the woff2 fonts 60 KB.  The icon sprite costs nothing: item sprite-inline
    already puts it in the page.
    Two hard parts, both decided before code:
      - it must be served from /sw.js to control "/", and today only /web/* and the two pages have a route, so
        the server half has to land before the page half - the ordering trap in LESSONS.md ("a web-app file that
        needs a matching server change breaks the running app the moment you save it").  The page's registration
        should tolerate the 404 the running server gives until it is restarted.
      - a service worker registers only in a secure context: http://localhost and https.  This box runs
        host 0.0.0.0, so the page opened from a LAN address over http cannot have it - "opens with no server"
        means on this PC, not from a phone.  Say so in the docs rather than letting it read as a promise.
    SHIPPED 2026-11-21, both halves.  serve/web/sw.js is network-first for the page and its own files and
    answers from the cache ONLY when the network call fails - that order is the whole design, because it keeps
    item asset-validator's promise ("edit app.js, reload, no restart") true while still opening the page with
    the port dead.  Online it also refreshes the cache on the way past, so an offline visit gets the files the
    server was actually running, not the ones from install time.  What it caches: "/", the three stylesheets,
    app.js, favicon, and the two woff2 fonts (measured: app.js 208 KB, app.css 36 KB, components.css 12 KB,
    tokens.css 4 KB, fonts 47 KB).  What it never touches: /v1/*, /health, /metrics, /settings, /config, /mcp,
    any POST, any other origin - answering one of those from a cache would look like a live reply.
    The server gained one route, /sw.js, and refuses /web/sw.js: a worker's scope is its own path, so a worker
    under /web/ could not control the page, and a second path is a way to get it wrong silently rather than a
    second way to get it right.  No-cache on the WORKER is deliberate - a long max-age there means an old
    worker serves an old app.js forever and nothing on screen says so.
    The page registers it in the start block, guarded on isSecureContext and on the key existing, with the
    rejection swallowed: a browser at a LAN address over http, a browser with no serviceWorker at all, and a
    server older than the file (404) are all normal and none of them is something the reader can act on.
    Tests: serve/test_static_cache.py class WorkerRoute (4 - served from the root as javascript, revalidates to
    a 304, no second path, the page route untouched); serve/test_service_worker.js (5 ok lines, new - it loads
    web/sw.js for real against a stub worker scope and drives install/activate/fetch); harness case 101 (92 ok
    lines - the path and scope, and the three unavailable cases).  Controls that bite: cache-first instead of
    network-first (the online case fails: a cached copy would be served and an edit would never show),
    cacheable() always true (the API gets intercepted), register() without the catch (an unhandled rejection),
    and dropping the isSecureContext guard.
    Still unverified: any of it in a browser - there is none on this box.  What to look for after the restart:
    DevTools > Application > Service Workers shows /sw.js, a reload with the server stopped still opens the
    chat, and the conversations are already there (IndexedDB, unaffected).
    TWO FALSE CLAIMS FIXED the same day, at the owner's ask.  With the server down the empty state still read
    "The model runs on this PC. Nothing leaves it." - never updated, because the /health fetch had failed - and
    an exported chat was headed with the built-in default model name, because a page opened from the worker's
    cache never gets a /health to ask.  Now a failed fetch says "The server is not running. The chats in this
    browser are still here.", and a successful /health remembers the name under localStorage key strata.model,
    which is what a page boots with when there is nothing to ask.  Harness case 103 (93 ok lines); controls:
    delete either half and its assertion fails.

`tools-block-empty`  A request asked for MCP tools and got none: instrumented and partly repaired, 2026-11-21. [py] PARTLY DONE
    What it looks like from the chat: an agent turn just stops.  The request ends on `stop` with plausible text,
    no tool call, and EVERY counter at zero - not an empty turn (content was delivered), not abandoned, not
    unread, not drift, not an unknown name.  Measured on this box 2026-11-21: one process served three requests
    whose records carry `tools: 0` and not a single `writing a tool call` line in the whole process - while the
    same process had printed `MCP server 'easyCMP': 31 tools` at start-up.  The hub was up; the prompt still had
    no tools block.
    Why it kills the turn: `use_mcp = bool(extra)` (serve/server.py, the strata_mcp branch) - an empty list means
    the request runs as if MCP were off, and a model with no tools block does not complain, it answers in prose.
    The hub's own print says "Its tools are left out; the chat works without them", which is true for a person
    chatting and false for an agent whose every turn is a tool call.
    Three ways to get there, and they need different fixes: no server ready (`routes()` empty); the
    conversation's pinned list (`strata_mcp_tools`, todo #mcp-live-toggles) matching none of the hub's current
    names - a renamed or removed tool, or a tool server that came back with a different list (the hub now prints
    31 tools where this repo's notes recorded 30, so the list HAS changed on this box); or every tool of every
    server turned off in the config.
    DONE 2026-11-21 - the instrument.  The handler now prints which of the three it was, naming each server's
    status and tool count, and counts `mcp_requests_without_tools` in /metrics.  Tests: serve/test_mcp.py class
    NoTools (2 - the failed-server reason, and the pinned-list-matched-nothing reason); control: delete the
    print and both fail.
    STILL OPEN: which of the three it was in that process (its records died with it), and what the server should
    DO about the other two.  Also done 2026-11-21, the one cause with a repair worth making: a pinned list that
    matches NOTHING now falls back to the hub's current list and says so - because 0 tools is already a different
    tools block from the one in that conversation's prompt, so the re-read the pinned list exists to prevent is
    happening either way, and the fallback costs nothing extra while keeping the turn able to call something.
    Test: serve/test_mcp.py NoTools.test_a_pinned_tool_list_that_matches_nothing_falls_back_to_the_current_one
    (the tool runs, and the "got NONE" line does NOT appear); control: delete the fallback and the request runs
    with no tools block while the log still says `fake=ready(7 tools)` - which is the whole fault in one line.
    Left open: no server ready (the handler already waits 10 s for one; is that enough, and should a request
    that gets none be refused rather than answered tool-less?), and every tool turned off in the config (that
    one is the user's choice and should stay a choice).

`unread-call-recovery`  Stop losing a tool call the model wrote: port upstream's parser fixes. [py] MEDIUM
    STEP 1 DONE 2026-11-19 - the drift instrument.  An agent chat died twice on this box with a round that
    delivered text, ended on stop, and ran no call, and item unread-call-instrument's counter said zero: the
    markup was not the template's envelope at all, so the envelope logic never saw it.  Measured against the real
    parser with tool_call_recovery on, before any of it was handled:
      - a function opener with a space where = should be   -> no call, no counter, delivered as text
      - the invoke dialect (Claude's form)                 -> no call, no counter, delivered as text
      - a parameter written as a named parameter tag       -> THE CALL RAN with {} arguments
      - a parameter written as an XML element              -> THE CALL RAN with {} arguments
      - two parameters, one of them named                  -> the call ran with one mangled value: the closer and
        the next parameter's name swallowed into the first value
    The last three are worse than the deaths: the tool runs, answers, and the conversation goes on as if it
    worked.  Nothing on the box said so.
    What shipped: OutputParser counts `drift` / `drift_kinds` (a round that declared tools, delivered no call, and
    ends on markup of that shape - DRIFT_MARKS, composed from the parser's own constants, never spelled out) and
    `empty_args` / `empty_args_names` (a call that fired with no arguments although the tool declares properties).
    serve/server.py prints both and totals them (drift_tool_markup, calls_with_empty_args).  Two things the tests
    pin, because they are what makes the numbers readable rather than noise: a quoted example - closed fence,
    tilde fence, inline code - is NOT drift (marks_outside_code walks the tail the same way the answer's fence
    rules do), a call swallowed by a fence that never closes is counted by unread and NOT again by drift, and a
    round that ran its call is not counted even if it then writes about the shape.  Tests:
    serve/test_reasoning_rescue.py class DriftMarkup (8), five controls, each mapped to the test that fails when
    the mechanism is removed - two of my first controls were insensitive (wrong edit, wrong test) and were
    rebuilt.  Needs a restart.
    FIFTH SHAPE, found from a live recording 2026-11-20 (the human: "I just had it die in another chat").  Every
    existing counter read zero for that turn - unread, drift, empty_args, abandoned, empty_turn, refused, rescued.
    The api_monitor record says it plainly: HTTP 200, reasoning 3,772 characters, output ZERO.  Output zero with
    no EMPTY TURN printed means `delivered` was set by something other than content, and the only thing that sets
    it besides content is a delivered tool_call.  Reproduced against the real loop with the fake hub: a call
    naming `fake__nothere` - neither in the request's tools nor in the hub's - comes back as
    `finish_reason: "tool_calls"` with NO MCP activity events at all, the loop breaks on `own_calls`, and the
    model never gets a second turn (engine prompts == 1).  The page never reads `delta.tool_calls` - it only ever
    writes them when rebuilding history (serve/web/app.js) - so nothing appears on screen.  That is the whole
    chain: parser fires, loop refuses, page ignores, chat dies silently.
      - DONE: run_with_mcp counts it (`unknown_tool_calls` in totals, one printed line naming the tool the model
        asked for) and distinguishes it from a tool the REQUEST declared, which is normal and must not count.
        Tests: serve/test_mcp.py class UnknownCall (3) - the notice and the total; the current broken behaviour
        pinned on purpose (no mcp events, finish_reason tool_calls, the only text is what came before the call,
        one engine turn) so it goes red the day the loop is fixed and must be rewritten, not deleted; and a
        client-declared tool is not counted.  Three controls bite (stop counting, drop the notice, loosen the
        gate).  Needs a restart.
      - THE RE-READ the owner noticed right after, asked about: not the fix, and the numbers say so.  That
        request re-used only 20,543 of 601,110 prompt tokens (3.4%) and read 580,567 - the break is at the
        system turn, where the tools block lives.  An errored tool round sits deep in a conversation, and a
        divergence there looks like the opposite: three other re-reads in the same window kept 79.9%, 65.2% and
        68.4% of the prefix and re-read the tail.  So something changed the tools block at that moment - a
        server added/removed/toggled (the note warns about exactly this), a server FINISHING its start, or the
        hub re-listing.  What was missing is the attribution, so it is now instrumented: the handler compares
        the tool names it rendered against the previous request and prints one line naming the count and who
        went and who arrived, and every /metrics request record carries `tools`.  Test:
        serve/test_mcp.py test_a_change_in_the_tool_list_is_announced_and_counted (a hub switch mid-conversation
        -> the notice, and the record going 7 -> 0); two controls bite.  Needs a restart.
      - THE FIX, done 2026-11-20 (asked: "make any fix you can").  A call naming neither the request's tools nor
        the hub's tools is now ANSWERED instead of ending the turn: it goes through the same call/result channel,
        and `hub.call` already produces exactly the right result for a name it has no server for - "error: there
        is no tool named X" - so the model sees it and gets another turn to pick the tool it meant.  Routed that
        way on purpose: the page replays any tool with a result (assistantMessages filters on
        `t.result != null && t.state !== "skipped"`), so the server's round_msg and the page's rebuild stay
        identical and the prompt prefix does not break - the hazard that made the retry idea (e05e6e0cc) expensive.
        A tool the REQUEST declared is untouched: it still goes out as a tool_calls delta for the client to
        answer.  Capped at two answered rounds per request (upstream #TOOLCALL-B caps it the same way) - with
        mcp.max_rounds at 900 in this config, an uncapped model inventing names would burn rounds for a long
        time; past the cap the call is shown as not run and the turn ends, as it used to.
        Tests: serve/test_mcp.py class UnknownCall (4) - the notice and the total; the error reaches the client
        as start/call/result with ok False and the model gets a third engine turn and ends with an answer; the cap
        (two answered, the third "not run", three counted); and a client-declared tool is not counted.  Four
        controls bite (refuse unknown names again, never merge them, drop the cap, drop the over-cap notice) - the
