        Also fixed in passing: UnknownCall inherited ToolLoop, so it silently re-ran ToolLoop's tests (45 tests
        here, not the 64 that inflation produced); the fixture is now LoopFixture and both classes use it.
        Needs a restart.
      - THE COST OF THAT FIX, found and fixed 2026-11-21 (the owner saw the re-read in the Monitor and asked for
        it).  The answered-error round DID cost a re-read, and the page was not at fault: run_with_mcp kept the
        calls it answers in two lists and joined them real-first (`calls += unknown`), while a client rebuilds a
        round in the order the calls ARRIVED (onTool pushes on the announcement, assistantMessages replays that
        array).  A round that wrote an unknown call BEFORE a real one therefore entered the history in a
        different order than the engine had been fed - the calls and their results both moved - and the exact-
        prefix reuse stopped matching at that round.  Measured in a real agent chat on this box: the request
        after such a round read 36,281 of its 87,463 prompt tokens (14.4 s) where its neighbours read 91 and
        876, and the client hung up while waiting (finish=disconnect).  Fixed: one ordered list (`answered`), so
        the round keeps the model's order; nothing else about the round changes.
        Test: serve/test_mcp_round_history.py test_a_mixed_round_keeps_the_order_the_model_wrote - the page's
        side is built from the events it was actually sent, in the CLIENT's shape, and read through
        openai_to_messages and the real template (the rule from the 2026-11-20 breaks).  Two controls bite: the
        old join fails the order assertion, and reversing the client's rebuild fails the prefix assertion.
        How it was found is in LESSONS.md ("a re-read inside one request is only in the engine log").
        Needs a restart.
      - THE CAP WAS WRONG, found by living with it 2026-11-21 (it killed this session's own turn).  The two
        answered rounds were counted PER REQUEST, and an agent turn is ONE request with thirty tool rounds in
        it: three slips across thirty rounds is ordinary work, not the name-inventing loop the cap guards
        against.  The third went to the over-cap branch, its call was shown as "not run", the loop broke, and
        the turn ended with no answer - the exact death this item is about, caused by the fix for it.
        Measured: `[strata] 3 call(s) named a tool that neither the request declared nor this server offers:
        easyCMP__fs_run_cmd`, in a request that had run ~30 real rounds and 268,485 prompt tokens.
        Fixed: the counter is a STREAK now - any round that runs a real tool resets it - so back-to-back
        invention still stops after a short streak, and a slip in a long working turn never does.  RAISED to
        five in a row the same day (the owner: "it won't hurt much, and it's better than dying") - and the
