        thinking (serve/server.py:4235), while this one opened after the thinking closed.  And it cost the
        turn, not the cache: the next pass read 17 tokens of 293,959.
      - THE SHAPE ANSWERED, 2026-11-21 - the owner chose this over leaving it ("I don't want it to stop"), and
        over re-running the pass, because a round is the one thing the server and the page already rebuild
        identically.  A call opened and never finished now gets the same treatment as a call for a name nobody
        offers: the loop adds the round - the call as far as it got, plus a result naming the parameter it
        stopped inside and saying nothing ran - and the model gets another pass to write it whole.  The parser
        already held every piece needed (the announced call is `scall`, and the scanner's state says where it
        stopped), so the change is one new event, `tool_cut`, carrying the call and the parameter name, and the
        loop answering it.  The call is NEVER run: control B showed why - with the guard removed the hub ran
        `fake__echo` with `{}`, the half-written arguments simply gone, which is the silent wrong-answer shape
        this item already documents twice.  Capped at BAD_CALL_STREAK rounds in a row (five), the same rule as
        the unknown-call answer, and an answered cut does not reset the streak - only a round that actually ran
        a tool does, which was a bug waiting to happen.  A cut-off call is always the last thing a pass wrote,
        so appending it preserves the order the model wrote the round in.
        The page needs no new code - a tool block with an error result is already recorded and replayed - and
        that is pinned rather than asserted (serve/test_chat_sidebar.js case 100), including the half that must
        NOT change: a call refused over the cap still stays out of the history.  Note for the next reader: that
        last assertion needs BOTH halves of the replay filter removed to fail (`round != null` and
        `state !== "skipped"` each exclude it alone), which is how I found the first control I wrote for it was
        measuring nothing.
        Tests: serve/test_mcp.py class CutCall (3) plus ToolLoop's rewritten #211 test;
        serve/test_mcp_round_history.py test_a_cut_off_call_becomes_a_round_both_sides_rebuild_the_same.
        Counted apart from the shape: totals `cut_tool_calls` and `cut_tool_calls_answered`.  Three controls
        bite: drop the parser's handover (back to `['start']` only, nothing delivered), drop the streak reset
        (the third cut in a working turn is refused), let the prepared call reach the hub (it runs with `{}`).
        The Anthropic path needed a guard for the new kind - LESSONS.md, "a 400 can be our bug" - and
        serve/test_server.py UnfinishedToolCall is what caught it; those five tests still pin the plain
        non-MCP path as it was.  Needs a restart.

