    not mention re-reading passed even with the narration restored; it now asserts the span is literally empty.
    THE PANEL COVERED ITS OWN BUTTON, so a second click could not close it (the human saw the cursor land under
    the panel's Undo button).  Three attempts, and the first two were wrong for the same reason.  (1) It used
    `r.top - min(h, space) - 4` with h = el.offsetHeight measured BEFORE the max-height was set; a 0 there puts
    the panel's top at the button's top.  (2) Park it, set the cap, measure, lift - still covered it in the
    browser, because the whole approach depends on measuring a rendered height that the browser reports
    differently than a test stub does.  (3) The fix that cannot fail is geometric: pin the edge NEAREST the
    button.  An upward panel gets `bottom = vh - r.top + 6` and no `top` at all, so its bottom edge is 6 px above
    the button whatever its content; a downward one gets `top = r.bottom + 6`.  max-height still caps it to the
    room and the panel scrolls inside that.  No offsetHeight read anywhere in the function, and the row menu
    shares it.
    Harness case 93 asserts the contract, including that four different content heights (40, 500, 9000, 0) all
    land the panel in exactly the same place - which is the whole point of pinning the near edge.  Control:
    pinning the far edge again fails "no top for an upward panel - its far edge is what is pinned".
    mdmine is now ON by default (the human asked, after typing _Never_ and seeing it literal - the other half of
