## 10. Done and shelved - kept by name, because serve/web/app.js, serve/web/app.css, serve/server.py,
    serve/test_* and LESSONS.md cite them in comments

`markdown-external-content-button`  A "Load external content" button for images and URLs the model typed. [ui] SHELVED 2026-11-20
    Asked for by the human, after Thunderbird's model: remote content is not loaded by default, and one button lets
    the reader allow it.  Shelved as low priority, with the reason kept so it is not re-argued later.  Today the
    renderer turns an image written in markdown into a plain link and fetches nothing, and the pictures that matter
    arrive through a tool and are displayed (item ai-image-visible).  What is at stake: text in a conversation may
    have come from a web page or a file the tools read, and a browser that fetches a URL that text names can call
    home or reach the local network.  A per-message opt-in is the right shape if it is ever built - the button keeps
    the default closed, which is exactly why the default is closed now.

`auto-scroll-big-block`  Auto-scroll stops following when a big block lands (a tool call, a table). [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    The human's diagnosis is correct, and the code shows why. `paint()` at
    serve/web/app.js:1491 does `updateAssistant(el, m, true); scrollDown();` - so
    `nearBottom()` measures the gap AFTER the DOM has already grown. A chunk that adds
    thousands of pixels in one frame leaves the reader more than 120 px from the bottom, so
    the follow switches itself off and never comes back.
    The thinking block already gets this right (app.js:1187 measures `nearEnd(body, 48)`
    before assigning `textContent`), which is why thinking follows and the chat does not.
    Fix: measure before mutating, the same way - `const follow = nearBottom(); ...update...;
    if (follow) s.scrollTop = s.scrollHeight;`. One line moved, plus a test that grows the
    content by more than the slack in one step.
    The slack of 120 px is also worth revisiting: a table row or a code fence is easily
    taller than that.

`sidebar-yesterday`  The sidebar's "Yesterday" is arithmetic, not a calendar day. [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    `groupName` (serve/web/app.js) does `Math.floor((Date.now() - t) / 86400000)`, so a chat
    from 23:00 yesterday read at 01:00 today is "Today", and nothing is ever "Yesterday"
    unless it is 24-48 hours old. The human's report is right.
    Fix: compare calendar dates for Today/Yesterday (local midnight, not UTC - the app shows
    local times elsewhere). "Previous 7 days" and "Previous 30 days" can stay arithmetic if
    preferred, but the same diff makes them calendar-correct for free.

`sidebar-quarter-groups`  Past 30 days, group the sidebar by calendar quarter, not "Older". [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    Decided 2026-10-24 with the human: after "Previous 30 days" the buckets are quarters -
    "2025 Q4", "2026 Q1", ... newest first - not a fixed count per group.  A count bucket
    re-flows every time a chat is added and labels nothing a reader can act on; quarters are
    stable and bound the heading count even across years.
    One function: extend groupName in serve/web/app.js, which already switched to calendar
    days (item sidebar-yesterday).  Replace the `return "Older"` branch with a quarter label
    from the chat's local year and Math.floor(month/3).  The five existing names and the
    function signature must stay - serve/test_chat_sidebar.py pins them by text.
    Test: drive renderSidebar with chats spread over several quarters and assert the headings
    come out newest-first.  Build the timestamps from local midnights like cases 14 and 51 do;
    Date.now() minus N days lands on the wrong calendar bucket depending on the hour.

