`recover-noop`  "recover" was a silent no-op for a request that asks low or medium effort. [py] DONE 2026-10-24
    focused_recovery_prompt (serve/server.py:193) looks only for the xhigh sentence. A request that asked low
    or medium carries that level's sentence instead, so the token subsequence is absent, the function returns
    None, and the loop is detected with "recover" configured while nothing is recovered. Measured in
    strata-slow-death.txt: a 63-token prompt at effort_position "start" has no xhigh sentence and returns
    None; at "end" the sentence is present and it returns 93 ids.
    Who was bitten: not this box's own web traffic (the app sends high, mapped to xhigh at
    chat_template.jinja:59-61), but any other client that asks low or medium - common as soon as a config or
    an app sets reasoning_effort low. This config sets no effort_position, so it is the default "start".
    FIXED: the splice now keys on the sentence actually in the prompt rather than on the level it assumes.
    server.py:193 - xhigh or medium sentence present -> replaced by the low one; no sentence at all (what the
    template does for medium, chat_template.jinja:66-70) -> the low one inserted at the head of the system
    turn, anchored on the role's ids and None rather than a blind splice if they are not a run; already low ->
    None, because there is nothing lower to ask for. The caller no longer does that in silence: server.py:4094
    says once that the recovery cannot lower this request, and it does not print the "resuming the same output"
    line for work it did not do.
    HOW TO CHECK IT WITHOUT AN ENGINE OR A GPU (the recipe that made the diagnosis credible, so the log file
    itself is not needed): render one small request with the server's own functions at each effort level, then
    call focused_recovery_prompt on the ids. At effort_position "start" with low or medium the xhigh sentence
    is absent and it returns None; at "end" the sentence is present and it returns a spliced prompt.
    strata-slow-death.txt is deliberately NOT committed: it is pasted live model output and carries the
    template's boundary tags as literal text, which is what cut two turns today. Everything worth keeping
    from it is in this item and in slow-death.

    Tests: serve/test_reasoning_loop_recovery.py 18 -> 22. Two controls, each failing on the assertion meant to
    catch it: swapping the original xhigh-only function back in fails the medium-insert test, and putting the
    silent caller back fails the log test. A first control (dropping "medium" from the levels tuple) PASSED,
    which is what showed the insert path already covered that case and the control was testing nothing.
    STILL OPEN: what to do when the request is already at low. It now logs and lets the thinking run to the
    budget, as before; ending it there instead (the "stop" behaviour) is the alternative and is not chosen.

